00:06:29.179 --> 00:06:34.399
China's Chang'e Five Samples formed only about 2 billion years ago, yet the rock still

00:06:34.399 --> 00:06:35.799
carries volcanic textures.

00:06:36.399 --> 00:06:41.139
Standard cooling models had the moon's interior frozen solid well before those lava could

00:06:41.139 --> 00:06:43.639
have formed, with the mantle cold and locked.

00:06:44.379 --> 00:06:48.959
As the moon travels around Earth, Earth's gravity raises tides in the lunar rock that

00:06:48.959 --> 00:06:50.339
repeat on a monthly cycle.

00:06:50.339 --> 00:06:57.899
That pole stretches and squeezes the interior again and again, and friction inside the rock turns that mechanical energy into heat.

00:06:58.459 --> 00:07:01.519
The way a paperclip grows warm when it is bent back and forth.

00:07:02.159 --> 00:07:09.139
Over billions of years, that steady input can keep pockets of the mantle above the melting point, even while the rest of the body cools.

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:13.219
Title heating reaches its peak when the moon orbits closer to Earth.

00:07:13.859 --> 00:07:18.019
Early in lunar history, the moon circled at roughly one-third its present distance,

00:07:18.019 --> 00:07:21.119
seeds, so the gravitational forces were far stronger.

00:07:21.659 --> 00:07:26.339
That extra energy likely sustained widespread melting for hundreds of millions of years.

00:07:26.939 --> 00:07:31.839
As the moon receded through tidal interactions, the heating rate declined but never vanished

00:07:31.839 --> 00:07:32.439
completely.

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:38.159
The Chang'e 5 basalts indicate that at least some regions stayed warm enough for volcanism

00:07:38.159 --> 00:07:40.919
until relatively recently in lunar history.

00:07:41.679 --> 00:07:46.799
Miyau Chen of the Chengdu University of Technology and colleagues published the tidal heating

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:52.279
explanation in the journal planet. The cumulative effect of monthly flexing can offset the

00:07:52.279 --> 00:07:56.759
expected cooling, and the process operates most efficiently in regions where rock

00:07:56.759 --> 00:08:02.239
properties allow greater dissipation of tidal energy into heat. We still do not know exactly

00:08:02.239 --> 00:08:07.359
how that heat was distributed through the Moon's interior. Current models disagree on whether

00:08:07.359 --> 00:08:13.019
tidal energy concentrated in a thin layer near the core mantle boundary, or spread more evenly

00:08:13.019 --> 00:08:17.959
through the mantle. Resolving the distribution requires new measurements of the moon's present

00:08:17.959 --> 00:08:23.019
day internal temperature profile, and the precise rate at which heat continues to escape from

00:08:23.019 --> 00:08:27.999
the surface today. Without those data, it is not possible to determine whether tidal heating

00:08:27.999 --> 00:08:33.719
alone accounts for the observed volcanic activity, or whether additional mechanisms also contributed.

